// 'each' headers the parser cannot turn into a loop.
//
// All of 'each' is parse-stage work: the parser rewrites the loop into a
// variable declaration and a 'while', so the header has to be well formed
// before there is anything for a later stage to look at. Each case sits in its
// own function, because the parser abandons the whole 'each' statement at the
// first thing it cannot read and re-reads what follows as a statement of its
// own.

// A loop variable name is required directly after 'each'. Giving up here leaves
// the parser on 'in', which is not the start of anything.
fn noVariable() {
  each in                //~ ErrorNoVar:8 "Missing variable name"
                         //~^ ErrorBadTerm:8 follow-on
  imm ok = 1
}

// 'in' is required after the loop variable, and the '{' that would have opened
// the body is then read as the start of a second statement on the line.
fn noIn() {
  each i 1 < 2 {         //~ ErrorBadTok:10 "Missing 'in'"
                         //~^ ErrorNoSemi:16 follow-on
  }
}

// Only the numeric range operators are rewritten into a loop. Everything else
// refeach.html documents -- a collection, a slice, a closure iterator -- has no
// iteration protocol behind it yet, so the loop and its body are dropped. That
// has to be said: dropping them in silence makes 'each' a statement that
// compiles and does nothing.
fn notARange() {
  each x in 7 {          //~ ErrorNotIterable:13 "only iterate over a numeric range"
  }
  each y in [1, 2] {     //~ ErrorNotIterable:13 "only iterate over a numeric range"
  }
}

// 'by' is recognized only after a range comparison. '5' is not one, so 'by' is
// never consumed and the parser is still looking for the body's '{' when it
// reaches it -- and the iterator is then reported as the non-range it is.
fn stepWithoutRange() {
  each i in 5 by 1 {     //~ ErrorNoLCurly:15
                         //~^ ErrorNotIterable:13 "only iterate over a numeric range"
  }
}

// 'each' is a statement and never an expression. The only way to write one in
// expression position is behind a lifetime label, which the expression parser
// does accept, so the check lives in the 'each' parser itself. It reports
// through errorMsg with no source position, so cases.toml carries it.
fn asExpression() {
  imm n = 'a: each i in 0 < 5 {
  }
}
